# THE WORK IS HERE. THE ROWS THAT DESCRIBE IT ARE NOT.
#
# The presence of this file is the declaration, in its PEER form: it says that
# landings in this repository are checked against a working record that lives
# somewhere else. It switches on the RichOS engine's
# guard-row-currency-commits.sh.
#
# WHY A POINTER RATHER THAN A CONTRACT
# ------------------------------------
# The record is a private record — it names people, decisions and unshipped
# work — and this repository is published. So the two cannot be one repository,
# and no single commit can ever touch both. That is exactly why "the merge that
# changes the work must also edit the row" could not be the rule as stated: the
# merge is here and the row is over there.
#
# What is checkable at this moment is simpler and stronger: at the instant this
# repository's tree changes, is the row that describes the changed work still
# pinned to the bytes it was written about? The engine reads the record from
# the repository named below, computes the tree this commit or merge is about
# to create, and refuses the landing if a row now describes something that is
# no longer there.
#
# IF THAT REPOSITORY IS NOT ON THIS MACHINE, NOTHING IS ENFORCED AND THE GUARD
# SAYS SO ON STDERR, EVERY TIME, AND BLOCKS NOTHING. Anyone who clones this
# public repository without the private sibling gets a loud stand-down, never a
# wedged commit. That is not a nicety; a guard that bricks a stranger's clone
# is a guard that gets deleted, and then it protects nobody.
#
# DRIFT BETWEEN THE TWO IS CHECKED. This pointer and the record's own artifact
# roots are two statements about one relationship. The record must declare a
# root that resolves back to this repository, or the contract is BROKEN and
# says so — half a contract enforces nothing.
#
# The full argument, the warrant grammar, the override decision and the honest
# list of what none of it can see are in the engine's
# scripts/lib/row-currency.sh.

# Relative to this repository's main checkout.
ROW_RECORD_REPO="../richos-hq"
